msgid "Create a catalog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:37
msgid "The prefix names the catalog's fields, such as ``:eds-number:``. It is 2 to 16 lowercase letters. ``init`` writes a complete catalog into a missing or empty folder:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:40
msgid "``discussion.toml``, the catalog's prefix, name, default author, lifecycle, and numbering;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:42
msgid "``conf.toml``, ``index.rst``, and ``workflow.rst``, an ordinary Guidedog project;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:43
msgid "``template/rfc-template.rst``, the template of new drafts;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:44
msgid "``registry.rst`` and ``bundle.rst``, the generated index;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:45
msgid "``records/XXXXX-eds-process.rst``, a draft of your review process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:47
msgid "Edit the process draft to say how your project decides. To start from your own documents, pass ``--config=FILE`` (a ``discussion.toml``), ``--process=FILE`` (the process text, from its title on), and ``--template=FILE`` (a record template with ``:eds-*:`` fields). ``--labels=a,b`` limits the labels records may carry. ``init`` checks the new catalog before it moves it into place, and never writes into a folder that has files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:54
msgid "Write and review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:57
msgid "Run the commands in the catalog folder, or in any folder inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:68
msgid "A new draft has the number ``XXXXX``. Promotion gives it the next number and opens it for discussion. A state change needs ``--reason``; ``committed`` also needs ``--evidence``. Add ``--dry-run`` to see a change without making it. These are the defaults; the next section changes them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:73
msgid "A record starts with its metadata, one field per line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:87
msgid "Authors and labels are JSON lists. ``last-updated`` may stand for ``updated``. A record may also carry ``category``, ``status``, and ``summary``; ``dsc`` keeps ``status`` in step with the state. HTML and PDF show the fields as Number, Title, Authors, Updated, Labels, and so on, with the lists as plain text."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:92
msgid "Configure the lifecycle and numbering"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:95
msgid "``discussion.toml`` holds the catalog's settings. ``init`` writes every one of them, with comments, and ``dsc config`` prints the settings in effect. A catalog without the file follows the defaults below, which are the process of Guidedog's own GDS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:132
msgid "``[workflow.states.NAME]`` defines a state: ``next`` lists the states the ``state`` command may move a record to, ``evidence = true`` asks for ``--evidence`` on a move into it, and ``status`` is the text a record's optional ``status`` field takes there. A state with no ``next`` is closed. Promotion is the only way from the draft state to the open state, because it gives the number; a draft may move without one only to the states its own ``next`` lists, such as ``abandoned``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:139
msgid "Numbers have at least ``width`` digits, padded with zeros. A number that needs more digits gets them, so 10000 follows 9999, and lists sort by value. Promotion numbers from ``start``; ``limit``, when set, is the highest number it gives. Changing ``start`` or ``limit`` later is safe. Changing ``width`` is not, once records are numbered: their numbers would no longer match it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:145
msgid "``dsc`` checks the file whenever it reads the catalog. A mistake names the line and what to write instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:157
msgid "The process draft that ``init`` writes describes the lifecycle you configured, with a table of the states and a diagram of the moves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:160
msgid "Build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:168
msgid "``dsc build`` writes the website to ``_build/html`` and one PDF per record to ``_build/pdf``. Naming a record writes only its PDF, to ``_build/selected``, and leaves the complete output alone. Warnings stop the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:172
msgid "``dsc`` runs the ``guidedog`` named by ``--guidedog=PATH``, then by ``DSC_GUIDEDOG``, then the one on ``PATH``. ``dscc`` builds with the Guidedog inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:175
msgid "When an update is interrupted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:178
msgid "Commands that change records hold the catalog's lock, ``.eds.lock``. An update of several files first writes a journal, ``.eds-journal.json``. If it stops part way, the next command says so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:187
msgid "Recovery puts back the files as they were. It refuses to overwrite a file someone edited since. ``dsc`` also checks each change before it writes anything: an empty or cut-short record, or an index without its generated region, is refused. When memory runs out, the command stops with exit status 3 and changes nothing it had not prepared whole."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:192
msgid "``dsc`` keeps only each record's metadata in memory, about a kilobyte a record, and reads a record whole only to change it. A catalog of 2,000 records of 100 KB each, 200 MB in all, is checked in a tenth of a second with less than 20 MB of memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:196
msgid "Use the library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:199
msgid "The three programs share one Odin package, ``lib/discussion``. Its `cheat sheet <https://github.com/insanai/guidedog/blob/main/lib/discussion/README.md>`_ shows how to load a catalog, plan a change, and apply it from your own program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:1
msgid "Document a codebase"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:4
msgid "A public name needs a contract. Explain its input, its result, its ownership, and the conditions under which it fails. A signature alone is not a manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:7
msgid "Static extraction architecture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:10
msgid "Guidedog extracts API documentation by parsing source files directly into abstract syntax trees. It never executes or imports user code, eliminating unintended side effects, external execution requirements, and foreign dependency issues:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:16
msgid "Code source trees are parsed into ASTs statically, transformed into virtual API documents, and integrated into cross-references and objects.inv."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:16
msgid "Guidedog static code documentation architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:61
msgid "Odin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:70
msgid "Guidedog reads Odin source through Odin's parser. It does not compile or run the documented package. The generated pages join the document graph and object inventory. The repository's separate API project is ``docs/api``. See :doc:`../odin` for directives, signatures, and options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:76
msgid "Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:84
msgid "Guidedog parses Python source rather than importing it. This avoids import side effects. It also means runtime-generated objects may be absent. Describe those objects explicitly when they are part of the public interface. See :doc:`../autodoc` for the exact discovery rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:89
msgid "Link across projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:92
msgid "Publish the generated ``objects.inv`` with the site. An intersphinx mapping can then resolve names from another project. Use explicit mappings for reproducible builds. See :doc:`../intersphinx`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:1
msgid "Publish to GitHub Pages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:4
msgid "Publish a Guidedog site to GitHub Pages in one of two ways. GitHub Actions can build and deploy it on each push, with released Guidedog and GitHub's official Pages actions, and no personal token. Or ``guidedog-pages`` can publish it from your own machine, whenever you ask or whenever the sources change. Neither needs Python or an Odin compiler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:12
msgid "Route 1 uses GitHub Actions CI/CD; Route 2 uses the local guidedog-pages publisher with a durable journal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:12
msgid "The two routes for publishing documentation to GitHub Pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:44
msgid "Build and deploy with GitHub Actions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:47
msgid "Prepare the project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:50
msgid "Create a project if you do not have one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:58
msgid "Commit the sources, configuration, templates, and static files. Keep generated output out of Git. In the repository's **Settings -> Pages**, choose **GitHub Actions** as the publishing source. Restrict the ``github-pages`` environment to ``main``; add approval rules if your team needs them. GitHub plan and organization policies may limit Pages availability."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:64
msgid "Add the workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:67
msgid "Save this as ``.github/workflows/docs-pages.yml``. It assumes a project in ``docs`` and a source branch named ``main``. Change those two names for your project. The build runs for pull requests too; only ``main`` can deploy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:154
msgid "The action commits are pinned. ``configure-pages`` uses the latest release checked for this guide, **v6.0.0**. When upgrading Guidedog, update both the version and the Linux amd64 archive's verified SHA-256. Review action updates separately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:158
msgid "If the project selects build plugins, install and register their trusted executables in the build job before running ``guidedog build``. Registrations on your computer are not copied to an Actions runner. Supply each plugin's project and configuration when registering it; qualification runs there too. Packaged plugins need no Odin compiler. See :doc:`../plugins` for the complete example. The deploy job uses GitHub's Pages action; it does not invoke a publishing plugin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:165
msgid "The build job can read sources. Only the deploy job receives Pages and OIDC permissions. GitHub supplies its short-lived credentials; do not add a personal token. A failed build prevents deployment. Pull-request content never enters the deploy job. Keep this workflow on ``pull_request``, not ``pull_request_target``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:170
msgid "Check the published site"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:173
msgid "Open the deployment URL shown by the completed workflow. For a project repository, it normally includes ``/REPOSITORY/``. Check navigation, search, images, language links, and fonts at that address. ``html_baseurl`` sets canonical metadata; it does not rewrite root-relative URLs in custom templates. Prefer relative asset links."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:178
msgid "For PDF downloads, build the book first and copy the PDF into ``build/site`` before the upload step. Assemble all language editions there too. Upload only the finished publication, never the repository root or a build cache. See :doc:`publish` for output paths and :doc:`../intl` for translation catalogs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:183
msgid "If deployment is refused, check the Pages source, the environment's allowed branches and approvals, and the deploy job's permissions. A successful upload is not a successful deployment. The official action reports the deployment result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:189
msgid "A site built from a private repository is not necessarily private. Check its Pages visibility before publishing confidential material."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:192
msgid "Publish from this machine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:195
msgid "``guidedog-pages`` publishes from your own computer instead: it builds the project with the installed ``guidedog``, and pushes the whole site to a branch of its own in one Git commit. GitHub Pages then deploys that branch. It comes with Guidedog in the release archives and the Homebrew formula, and needs no Git, Python, or Odin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:200
msgid "Use one route per site. A repository has one Pages source: GitHub Actions or a branch. ``guidedog-pages`` refuses to publish while Pages deploys from Actions or from another branch, and switching is a deliberate ``setup`` step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:204
msgid "Set it up once"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:207
msgid "Write ``pages.toml`` beside your project. It names the repository, the branch, and the credential profile; it holds no secret, so you can commit it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:214
msgid "Store a token for the profile. Make a fine-grained personal access token for the repository with **Contents: read and write**, and **Pages: read** so that publication can confirm the deployment (**Pages: write** and **Administration: write** too if you want ``setup`` to configure Pages). Paste it when asked; it is not shown, and it goes to the macOS Keychain, the Windows Credential Manager, or the Secret Service of a Linux desktop, never to a file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:226
msgid "``auth login`` signs in with a code instead (GitHub's device flow), for a GitHub App or OAuth App you registered with the device flow enabled: ``guidedog-pages auth login --client-id=YOUR_APP_ID``. Guidedog ships without an app of its own."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:231
msgid "Authorize the target on this machine, and check that everything is ready:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:238
msgid "``bind`` shows the account, the repository with its GitHub ID, the branch, and the Pages settings, and asks before it records them. Publication goes only to a bound target: a ``pages.toml`` that names another repository, a repository that was renamed, transferred, or made again, or another account's token is refused until you bind again. ``bind`` refuses a branch that holds work Guidedog did not publish; ``--adopt`` lets the next publication replace it, after you have checked. The branch must not be the repository's default branch. ``doctor`` checks everything and changes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:247
msgid "Publish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:255
msgid "``publish`` builds the project with warnings as errors (``strict = true``), and sets ``html_baseurl`` to ``site_url``. It reads the branch, lists what will be added, changed, and removed, and sends one push that holds only the files the branch lacks. GitHub moves the branch only if it still has the commit that was read, so a publication never overwrites a newer one; if another publication got there first, run the command again. A ``.nojekyll`` file is added, so folders such as ``_static`` are served. Then it waits, up to ``wait_seconds``, for GitHub Pages to deploy that commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:264
msgid "The first publication makes the branch. GitHub usually starts deploying a new ``gh-pages`` branch by itself; otherwise ``publish`` ends with ``pages.no-site``. Then run ``guidedog-pages setup``, which asks before it points GitHub Pages at the branch, or choose **Deploy from a branch**, your branch, **/ (root)** in **Settings -> Pages**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:269
msgid "The exit status says what happened: 0 when the site is live or was already published, 1 when nothing was published, 6 when GitHub accepted the publication but has not confirmed the deployment yet, and 7 when it is not known whether the branch moved. Check later with ``guidedog-pages status``. If a publication lost its answer from GitHub, ``guidedog-pages recover`` reads the branch and settles it; it never moves the branch back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:276
msgid "To publish a site you build yourself, such as a site with several languages and PDF books assembled by a script, write ``site = \"build/site\"`` instead of ``project``; the folder is copied and checked before it is published."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:280
msgid "Publish on every change"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:283
msgid "``guidedog-pages watch`` publishes once, then again whenever the sources change and have been quiet for ``debounce_ms`` (at most ``max_wait_ms`` after the first change). A failed build or publication is reported, and the published site stays as it was. Ctrl+C stops it. It watches files on this machine; to publish after a push to GitHub, use the Actions route above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:289
msgid "A scheduler can keep it running. On macOS, a launch agent in ``~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.example.docs.plist``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:310
msgid "On Linux, a systemd user service, ``~/.config/systemd/user/docs-pages.service``, started with ``systemctl --user enable --now docs-pages``. A user service reaches the desktop's Secret Service; on a server without one, give the token with ``--credential=env`` and an ``Environment=GUIDEDOG_PAGES_TOKEN=...`` drop-in readable only by you:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:329
msgid "On Windows, a scheduled task at logon, so that the Credential Manager is available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:338
msgid "Tokens and automation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:341
msgid "``--credential=env`` takes the token from ``GUIDEDOG_PAGES_TOKEN`` for one command, and never stores it; ``GUIDEDOG_PAGES_CREDENTIAL=env`` chooses the same for runs that cannot take the option, such as ``guidedog publish``. Without either, the variable is ignored. A missing or locked credential store is reported; the token is never written to a plain file instead. ``auth logout`` deletes the stored token; revoke it at GitHub too, under **Settings -> Developer settings**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:348
msgid "Through guidedog publish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:351
msgid "``guidedog-pages`` is also a publisher for ``guidedog publish``, which builds the project with Guidedog's own engine and hands the frozen site to it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:359
msgid "Sign in, bind, and set up with ``guidedog-pages`` itself, or through ``guidedog plugin run pages -- auth import``. See :doc:`../plugins`."
msgstr ""

